    <string name="tarn_connection_lab_log_debug">Debug</string>
    <string name="tarn_connection_lab_log_trace">Trace</string>
    <string name="tarn_connection_lab_open_logs">Open log</string>
    <string name="tarn_connection_lab_open_logs_hint">Live core output at the level chosen above</string>
    <string name="tarn_logs_title">Log</string>
    <string name="tarn_servers_unmanaged">Settings do not apply — delete and add again</string>
    <string name="tarn_servers_insecure_tls">Server certificate is not verified — this traffic can be intercepted</string>
    <string name="tarn_shield_always_on">System always-on VPN</string>
    <string name="tarn_shield_always_on_desc">The toggle above only holds while the service is alive. If it crashes or the phone reboots, traffic goes out directly. Only the system setting closes that: turn on "Always-on VPN" and "Block connections without VPN". Open Android settings</string>
    <string name="tarn_connection_lab_ipv4_only_warning">All traffic goes over IPv4, and apps are told the name has no IPv6 at all. Keep "Send the hostname to the server" on alongside it, or some Google services answer "not available in your region".</string>
    <string name="tarn_connection_lab_send_hostname">Send the hostname to the server</string>
    <string name="tarn_connection_lab_send_hostname_desc">What Xray and v2rayNG do: the server resolves the name and picks the region, which is what Gemini needs to avoid "not available in your region". YouTube is the exception — resolved on the phone, or Music region-blocks. Turn off only if a server rejects domain destinations</string>
    <string name="tarn_connection_lab_no_v6_warning">Diagnostic mode. AAAA records are served as usual, but IPv6 destinations are refused by the route table, so traffic rides IPv4 — fast.com will show IPv4. The point is to separate "traffic over IPv4" from "the client was told there is no IPv6". If Gemini works here, the second half is the culprit.</string>
    <string name="tarn_connection_lab_debug_warning">Debug logging may expose visited domains in local logs and increases battery use. Disable it after testing.</string>

